.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x + 3y &= -10 \\\\\n2x - y &= 6 \\\\\n2y &= (-10 + 6) \\\\\n2y &= -4 \\\\\ny &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + (-3)(-2) &= 10 \\\\\n2x &= 4 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(2,-2)$." "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$, then we will get a $-4x$ term that can cancel with the $4x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - 3y &=10 && \\text{multiply by } -2 \\\\ 4x - 2y &=12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4x + 6y &= -20 \\\\\n4x - 2y &= 12 \\\\\n4y &= (-20 + 12) \\\\\n4y &= -8 \\\\\ny &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + (-3)(-2) &= 10 \\\\\n2x &= 4 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(2,-2)$.\n\nThe corresponding lines intersect at exactly one point."] [:db/cas 17592186529294 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x + y &=0 \\\\ -3x + 5y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x + 2y &=0 \\\\ -3x + 5y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186529294 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $1$, then we will get a $3x$ term that can cancel with the $-3x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x + y &=0 && \\mid \\cdot (1) \\\\ -3x + 5y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + y &= 0 \\\\\n-3x + 5y &= -2 \\\\\n6y &= (0 - 2) \\\\\n6y &= -2 \\\\\ny &= - \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + (1)\\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right) &= 0 \\\\\n3x &= \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{1}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(\\frac{1}{9},- \\frac{1}{3})$." "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $2$, then we will get a $-6x$ term that can cancel with the $6x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x + 2y &=0 \\\\ -3x + 5y &=-2 && \\text{multiply by } 2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x + 2y &= 0 \\\\\n-6x + 10y &= -4 \\\\\n12y &= (0 - 4) \\\\\n12y &= -4 \\\\\ny &= - \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x + (2)\\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right) &= 0 \\\\\n6x &= \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{1}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(\\frac{1}{9},- \\frac{1}{3})$.\n\nThe corresponding lines intersect at exactly one point."] [:db/cas 17592186529294 :question/difficulty 17592186045485 :question.difficulty/moderate] [:db/cas 17592186529306 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x - y &=8 \\\\ 3x + y &=-6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x - y &=8 \\\\ 6x + 2y &=-12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186529306 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $1$, then we will get a $-3x$ term that can cancel with the $3x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x - y &=8 && \\mid \\cdot (1) \\\\ 3x + y &=-6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x - y &= 8 \\\\\n3x + y &= -6 \\\\\n0 &= (8 - 6) \\\\\n0 &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=2$ is *always false*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *no solutions*." "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $2$, then we will get a $-6x$ term that can cancel with the $6x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x - y &=8 && \\text{multiply by } 2 \\\\ 6x + 2y &=-12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-6x - 2y &= 16 \\\\\n6x + 2y &= -12 \\\\\n0 &= (16 - 12) \\\\\n0 &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=4$ is *always false*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *no solutions*.\n\nThe corresponding lines are parallel and do not intersect."] [:db/cas 17592186529306 :question/difficulty 17592186045485 :question.difficulty/moderate] [:db/cas 17592186529316 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x - 5y &=-12 \\\\ x - 3y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x - 5y &=-12 \\\\ 2x - 6y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186529316 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $1$, then we will get a $-x$ term that can cancel with the $x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x - 5y &=-12 && \\mid \\cdot (1) \\\\ x - 3y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x - 5y &= -12 \\\\\nx - 3y &= -2 \\\\\n-8y &= (-12 - 2) \\\\\n-8y &= -14 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{7}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x + (-5)\\left(\\frac{7}{4}\\right) &= -12 \\\\\n-x &= - \\frac{13}{4} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{13}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(\\frac{13}{4},\\frac{7}{4})$." "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $2$, then we will get a $-2x$ term that can cancel with the $2x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x - 5y &=-12 && \\text{multiply by } 2 \\\\ 2x - 6y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x - 10y &= -24 \\\\\n2x - 6y &= -4 \\\\\n-16y &= (-24 - 4) \\\\\n-16y &= -28 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{7}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x + (-5)\\left(\\frac{7}{4}\\right) &= -12 \\\\\n-x &= - \\frac{13}{4} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{13}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(\\frac{13}{4},\\frac{7}{4})$.\n\nThe corresponding lines intersect at exactly one point."] [:db/cas 17592186529316 :question/difficulty 17592186045485 :question.difficulty/moderate] [:db/cas 17592186529320 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - y &=4 \\\\ 2x - y &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x - 2y &=8 \\\\ 2x - y &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186529320 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-1$, then we will get a $-2x$ term that can cancel with the $2x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - y &=4 && \\mid \\cdot (-1) \\\\ 2x - y &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x + y &= -4 \\\\\n2x - y &= 4 \\\\\n0 &= (-4 + 4) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=0$ is *always true*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *infinitely many solutions*." "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $-2$, then we will get a $-4x$ term that can cancel with the $4x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x - 2y &=8 \\\\ 2x - y &=4 && \\text{multiply by } -2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 2y &= 8 \\\\\n-4x + 2y &= -8 \\\\\n0 &= (8 - 8) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=0$ is *always true*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nThe corresponding lines coincide."] [:db/cas 17592186561213 :question/problem "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{alignedat}{2}\nl_{1}:\\quad & 5x - 2y &{}={}& -1 \\\\[6pt]\nl_{2}:\\quad & -5x + 2y &{}={}& 1\n\\end{alignedat}\n$$\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}." "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{alignedat}{2}\nl_{1}:\\quad & 5x - 2y &{}={}& -1 \\\\[6pt]\nl_{2}:\\quad & -10x + 4y &{}={}& 2\n\\end{alignedat}\n$$\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186561213 :question/worked-solution "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x - 2y &=-1 \\\\ -5x + 2y &= 1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. First, we solve for $y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x - 2y &=-1 \\\\\n-2y &= -5x - 1 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{-5x - 1}{-2} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{5}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5x + 2y &= 1 \\\\\n-5x + 2\\left(\\frac{5}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2}\\right) &= 1 \\\\\n-5x + 5x + 1 &= 1 \\\\\n1 &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a statement that's always true. Therefore, we conclude that the system has infinitely many solutions.\n\nConsequently, the lines intersect at infinitely many points (i.e., they coincide)." "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x - 2y &=-1 \\\\ -10x + 4y &= 2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. First, we solve for $y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x - 2y &=-1 \\\\\n-2y &= -5x - 1 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{-5x - 1}{-2} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{5}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-10x + 4y &= 2 \\\\\n-10x + 4\\left(\\frac{5}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2}\\right) &= 2 \\\\\n-10x + 10x + 2 &= 2 \\\\\n2 &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a statement that's always true. Therefore, we conclude that the system has infinitely many solutions.\n\nConsequently, the lines intersect at infinitely many points (i.e., they coincide)."] [:db/cas 17592186640186 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x + y &=-3 \\\\ 3x + y &=11\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x + 2y &=-6 \\\\ 3x + y &=11\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186640186 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-1$, then we will get a $-3x$ term that can cancel with the $3x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x + y &=-3 && \\text{multiply by } -1 \\\\ 3x + y &=11\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x - y &= 3 \\\\\n3x + y &= 11 \\\\\n0 &= (3 + 11) \\\\\n0 &= 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=14$ is *always false*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *no solutions*.\n\nThe corresponding lines are parallel and do not intersect." "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $-2$, then we will get a $-6x$ term that can cancel with the $6x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x + 2y &=-6 \\\\ 3x + y &=11 && \\text{multiply by } -2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x + 2y &= -6 \\\\\n-6x - 2y &= -22 \\\\\n0 &= (-6 - 22) \\\\\n0 &= -28\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=-28$ is *always false*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *no solutions*.\n\nThe corresponding lines are parallel and do not intersect."] [:db/cas 17592186640186 :question/difficulty 17592186045485 :question.difficulty/moderate] [:db/cas 17592186529261 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x + y &=6 \\\\ x - 2y &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x + y &=6 \\\\ x - 2y &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186529261 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $2$, then we will get a $2x$ term that can cancel with the $-2x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x + y &=6 \\\\ x - 2y &=6 && \\mid \\cdot (2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x + y &= 6 \\\\\n2x - 4y &= 12 \\\\\n-3y &= (6 + 12) \\\\\n-3y &= 18 \\\\\ny &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x + (1)(-6) &= 6 \\\\\n-2x &= 12 \\\\\nx &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(-6,-6)$." "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $2$, then we will get a $2x$ term that can cancel with the $-2x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x + y &=6 \\\\ x - 2y &=6 && \\mid \\cdot (2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x + y &= 6 \\\\\n2x - 4y &= 12 \\\\\n-3y &= (6 + 12) \\\\\n-3y &= 18 \\\\\ny &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x + (1)(-6) &= 6 \\\\\n-2x &= 12 \\\\\nx &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(-6,-6)$."] [:db/cas 17592186529269 :answer-field/correct 17592186529312 17592186529312] [:db/cas 17592186529265 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x + 4y &=-9 \\\\ 2x - y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x + 4y &=-9 \\\\ 2x - y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTh